  "query_preview": "..(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 437 AM EST SUN NOV 8 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Monday night with dry weather, conditions \nthat will last through the better part of Wednesday. Synoptically, \nsplit flow exists to open the period, with the northern jet stream \nnear the US-Canada border and the sub-tropical jet across Mexico and \nthe Gulf Coast. Surface high pressure will remain in the vicinity, \nlocated in the Tennessee and lower Mississippi valleys. Conditions \nwill remain favorable at night for good radiational cooling, \nespecially in decoupled valleys, with decent ridge-valley splits \nunder mostly clear skies and light winds. With the NBM exhibiting a \nrecent cold bias during strong decoupling setups, blended in the \nRRFS for both Monday and Tuesday nights to nudge minimum \ntemperatures up slightly while still preserving realistic ridge- to-\nvalley splits. Monday night, valleys and hollows could fall into the \nlower 30s, while mid to upper 30s remain along ridge tops. Areas of \nfrost will form in the coldest spots, while patchy river valley fog \nwill also develop.\n\nBy Tuesday, a strong upper-level cyclone will move into the Gulf of \nAlaska and Pacific Northwest region. While a ridge builds just \ndownstream over the Rockies and Intermountain West, multiple waves \nof energy over the northern Plains and Southeast Canada will evolve \ninto a deep longwave trough by midweek. Another shortwave over the \nCentral Plains on Tuesday will advect warmer air into the region \nboth Tuesday and Wednesday. As a result, temperatures each afternoon \nwill climb into the mid to upper 60s (5 to 10 degrees above seasonal \nnormals). Tuesday night will also feature ridge-valley splits, with \nthe coldest valleys dropping into the mid to upper 30s while ridge \ntops remain in the upper 30s to low 40s. Patchy frost may form in \nthe coldest spots, while areas of river valley fog will also \ndevelop. As mentioned previously, a vigorous northern-stream \nshortwave diving out of western Canada will carve out a deep, \nanomalous longwave trough over the eastern third of the CONUS by mid-\nweek. An attendant cold front will sweep through the Commonwealth \nlate Wednesday into Thursday, bringing increasing clouds and \nincreasing rain chances.\n\nThe core of the upper trough is modeled to move just north of the \narea Thursday into Friday, pushing 500 mb heights below the 5th \nclimatological percentile and ushering in a brisk Canadian air mass \non breezy northwest winds. Highs Friday and Saturday may struggle to \nclimb out of the 40s, with overnight lows potentially dropping into \nthe upper 20s and lower 30s. With model soundings suggesting steep \nlow-level lapse rates alongside northwest upslope flow, lingering \nshowers could mix with or transition to the first flurries of the \nseason across the higher terrain of southeast Kentucky Thursday \nnight into Friday before surface high pressure restores dry \nconditions for the weekend.",
